    "text": [
      "'Twas grav'd on the Stone of Destiny,",
      "In letters four, and letters three;",
      "And ne'er did the King of the Gulls go by",
      "But those awful letters scar'd his eye;",
      "For he knew that a Prophet Voice had said",
      "\"As long as those words by man were read,",
      "The ancient race of the Gulls should ne'er",
      "One hour of peace or plenty share.\"",
      "But years and years successive flew",
      "And the letters still more legible grew, --",
      "At top, a T, an H, an E,",
      "And underneath, D. E. B. T.",
      "Some thought them Hebrew, -- such as Jews,",
      "More skill'd in Scrip than Scripture use;",
      "While some surmis'd 'twas an ancient way",
      "Of keeping accounts, (well known in the day",
      "Of the fam'd Didlerius Jeremias,",
      "Who had thereto a wonderful bias,)",
      "And prov'd in books most learnedly boring,",
      "'Twas called the Pontick way of scoring.",
      "Howe'er this be, there never were yet",
      "Seven letters of the alphabet,",
      "That, 'twixt them form'd so grim a spell,",
      "Or scar'd a Land of Gulls so well,",
      "As did this awful riddle-me-ree",
      "Of T.H.E.D.E.B.T.",
      "Hark! - it is struggling Freedom's cry;",
      "\"Help, help, ye nations, or I die;",
      "'Tis freedom's fight, and on the field",
      "Where I expire, your doom is seal'd.\"",
      "The Gull-King hears the awakening call,",
      "He hath summon'd his Peers and Patriots all,",
      "And he asks, \"Ye noble Gulls, shall we",
      "Stand basely by at the fall of the Free,",
      "Nor utter a curse, nor deal a blow?\"",
      "And they answer, with voice of thunder, \"No.\"",
      "Out fly their flashing swords in the air! -",
      "But, -- why do they rest suspended there?",
      "What sudden blight, what baleful charm,",
      "Hath chill'd each eye and check'd each arm?",
      "Alas! some withering hand hath thrown",
      "The veil from off that fatal stone,",
      "And pointing now, with sapless finger,",
      "Showeth where dark those letters linger, --",
      "Letters four, and letters three,",
      "T.H.E. D.E.B.T.",
      "At sight thereof, each lifted brand",
      "Powerless falls from every hand;",
      "In vain the Patriot knits his brow, --",
      "Even talk, his staple, fails him now.",
      "In vain the King like a hero treads,",
      "His Lords of the Treasury shake their heads;",
      "And to all his talk of \"brave and free\",",
      "No answer getteth His Majesty",
      "But \"T.H.E. D.E.B.T.\"",
      "In short, the whole Gull nation feels",
      "The're fairly spell-bound, neck and heels;",
      "And so, in the face of the laughing world,",
      "Must e'en sit down, with banners furled,",
      "Adjourning all their dreams sublime",
      "Of glory and war to -- some other time."
    ],
    "fulltext": "'Twas grav'd on the Stone of Destiny,\nIn letters four, and letters three;\nAnd ne'er did the King of the Gulls go by\nBut those awful letters scar'd his eye;\nFor he knew that a Prophet Voice had said\n\"As long as those words by man were read,\nThe ancient race of the Gulls should ne'er\nOne hour of peace or plenty share.\"\nBut years and years successive flew\nAnd the letters still more legible grew, --\nAt top, a T, an H, an E,\nAnd underneath, D. E. B. T.\nSome thought them Hebrew, -- such as Jews,\nMore skill'd in Scrip than Scripture use;\nWhile some surmis'd 'twas an ancient way\nOf keeping accounts, (well known in the day\nOf the fam'd Didlerius Jeremias,\nWho had thereto a wonderful bias,)\nAnd prov'd in books most learnedly boring,\n'Twas called the Pontick way of scoring.\nHowe'er this be, there never were yet\nSeven letters of the alphabet,\nThat, 'twixt them form'd so grim a spell,\nOr scar'd a Land of Gulls so well,\nAs did this awful riddle-me-ree\nOf T.H.E.D.E.B.T.\nHark! - it is struggling Freedom's cry;\n\"Help, help, ye nations, or I die;\n'Tis freedom's fight, and on the field\nWhere I expire, your doom is seal'd.\"\nThe Gull-King hears the awakening call,\nHe hath summon'd his Peers and Patriots all,\nAnd he asks, \"Ye noble Gulls, shall we\nStand basely by at the fall of the Free,\nNor utter a curse, nor deal a blow?\"\nAnd they answer, with voice of thunder, \"No.\"\nOut fly their flashing swords in the air! -\nBut, -- why do they rest suspended there?\nWhat sudden blight, what baleful charm,\nHath chill'd each eye and check'd each arm?\nAlas! some withering hand hath thrown\nThe veil from off that fatal stone,\nAnd pointing now, with sapless finger,\nShoweth where dark those letters linger, --\nLetters four, and letters three,\nT.H.E. D.E.B.T.\nAt sight thereof, each lifted brand\nPowerless falls from every hand;\nIn vain the Patriot knits his brow, --\nEven talk, his staple, fails him now.\nIn vain the King like a hero treads,\nHis Lords of the Treasury shake their heads;\nAnd to all his talk of \"brave and free\",\nNo answer getteth His Majesty\nBut \"T.H.E. D.E.B.T.\"\nIn short, the whole Gull nation feels\nThe're fairly spell-bound, neck and heels;\nAnd so, in the face of the laughing world,\nMust e'en sit down, with banners furled,\nAdjourning all their dreams sublime\nOf glory and war to -- some other time.",
